This document provides a concise overview of the Machine Learning and Data Analysis techniques mastered throughout the course projects, highlighting proficiency in model development, optimization, and rigorous analysis across diverse applications.

Core Machine Learning & Statistical Modeling

    Supervised Learning: Comprehensive experience in both Regression (using Linear, Ridge, Lasso, SVR, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting) and Classification (using k-Nearest Neighbors, SVM, Logistic Regression, and Random Forest). Projects included solving complex binary classification problems and performing Weighted Non-Linear Least Squares fitting for statistical modeling.

    Unsupervised Learning: Applied Clustering algorithms like K-Means (tuned via the Elbow Method) and DBSCAN (tuned via ϵ and min_samples) to identify natural groupings. Expertise in Anomaly Detection using One-Class SVM and Isolation Forest to validate dataset integrity.

    Scientific Modeling: Utilized Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) principles to create modular, reusable code architectures for complex physics calculations, demonstrating encapsulation and modularity.

Optimization and Robust Workflow Design

    Hyperparameter Tuning: Standardized model optimization using Grid Search Cross-Validation (CV) to ensure robust selection of optimal parameters for all major models (SVM, Random Forest, etc.).

    ML Pipelines: Implemented Pipelines to consistently combine preprocessing steps (like Standardization/Normalization) with modeling, a critical practice to prevent data leakage during cross-validation.

    Regularization: Explored the effects of L1 (Lasso) and L2 (Ridge) regularization, demonstrating how L1 provides sparsity (automatic feature selection) and L2 stabilizes model coefficients.

    Advanced Optimization: Applied Bayesian Optimization (BO) with a Random Forest surrogate model to efficiently navigate high-dimensional search spaces for material synthesis, incorporating scientific constraints to guide the search.

Performance Analysis and Model Interpretation

    Evaluation Metrics: Proficiently used a range of metrics: Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE) for regression, Accuracy, Precision, and Recall (with focus on minimizing False Negatives in high-stakes problems), and the Chi-Squared (χ2) statistic for scientific curve fitting.

    Feature Importance: Employed both a Brute Force Forward Selection algorithm (performance-driven MAE reduction) and the advanced technique SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) for model transparency and feature ranking.

    Model Validation: Utilized Davies-Bouldin Index and Calinski-Harabasz Index for quantitative cluster validation. Generated Classification Reports, Confusion Matrices, and ROC Curves for comprehensive classification assessment.

    Uncertainty Quantification: Visualized model stability by using Monte Carlo sampling and the covariance matrix to plot model uncertainty boundaries around fitted statistical curves.
